k connection to 10.10.10.5:80 while performing system reconnaissance via WMI queries.","findings":[{"technique_id":"T1685.002","name":"Disable or Modify Cloud Log","parent":"Disable or Modify Tools","tactics":["Defense Impairment"],"behavior":"PowerShell process disabled script block logging by modifying the cached group policy settings in memory to prevent logging of subsequent commands.","slot":0,"reasoning":"The PowerShell script directly modifies the cached group policy settings in memory to disable script block logging by setting EnableScriptBlockLogging and EnableScriptBlockInvocationLogging to 0. This is defense evasion targeting PowerShell logging specifically, not Windows Event Log or authentication mechanisms. T1562.008 (Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Tools) covers disabling logging and monitoring tools.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E038","field":"ScriptBlockText","quote":"$1FE7['ScriptB'+'lockLogging']['EnableScriptB'+'lockLogging']=0;$1Fe7['ScriptB'+'lockLogging']['EnableScriptBlockInvocationLogging']=0","ok":true},{"event_id":"E038","field":"ScriptBlockText","quote":"GetFie`LD('cachedGroupPolicySettings','N'+'onPublic,Static')","ok":true}],"attempts":1},{"technique_id":"T1033","name":"System Owner/User Discovery","parent":null,"tactics":["Discovery"],"behavior":"PowerShell executed WMI queries to enumerate network adapter configuration and operating system details for system reconnaissance.","slot":2,"reasoning":"The evidence shows PowerShell executing whoami to identify the current user, which is system owner/user discovery. While the hypothesis claims WMI queries for network and OS enumeration, the actual logs only demonstrate whoami execution\u2014a direct user discovery technique\u2014not network adapter or OS detail enumeration via WMI.","evidence":[{"event_id":"E047","field":"Payload","quote":"Invoke-Expression\" ParameterBinding(Invoke-Expression): name=\"Command\"; value=\"whoami\"","ok":true},{"event_id":"E049","field":"CommandLine","quote":"C